<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    ============================================================
    bg_avatar_initial — circular initials-avatar background
    ===
    Referenced by:
      - item_conversation.xml  (tv_initials)
      - message-item layouts    (tv_sender_initials, when added)
      - fragment_profile.xml    (tv_profile_initials, when added)
      - item_user.xml           (tv_user_initials, when added)

    Consumed by UiUtils.resolveAvatarColor(context, uid) at
    bind time. The adapter calls:

        ViewCompat.setBackgroundTintList(
            tvInitials,
            ColorStateList.valueOf(UiUtils.resolveAvatarColor(context, uid)));

    Do NOT call setBackgroundResource / setBackgroundColor on a
    view that uses this drawable — that would replace the shape
    entirely and lose the circular outline. Tint is the only
    supported modification path.

    Why an <oval> shape, not a <vector> circle path:
      - An oval in a square bounds view is a perfect circle and
        costs one draw call. A vector circle path costs the same
        but requires the consumer to also apply the tint to the
        path, which is more error-prone in Java.
      - Shape drawables respond to setBackgroundTintList with a
        single line; vector path tinting requires DrawableCompat
        wiring and recycling considerations. The shape is the
        simpler primitive and does exactly what we need.

    Why white as the solid color, not transparent:
      - The shape drawable's solid is the color that
        setBackgroundTintList replaces. If the solid were
        transparent, the tint replacement would still work (tint
        is a color filter), but the ShapeDrawable's own
        self-draw would produce nothing visible if tinting is
        ever skipped — e.g. during a layout inflation preview in
        an editor, or if a future bug causes the tint to be
        omitted. White is the "safe fallback" that produces a
        valid, visible circle even without tinting (the letter
        would be white-on-white, but the shape would still be
        there and easy to spot in screenshots during debugging).
      - Do NOT change the solid to one of the @color/avatar_*
        values. That would hardcode one palette color and the
        tint would then be layered over it — producing a subtle
        color shift in the final rendering (e.g. #F2789F tinted
        over a #5DA9E9 base becomes a muted purple, not the
        intended pink). White is the only correct base for
        runtime tinting.

    Why no <stroke>:
      - The white text needs to be visible against each palette
        color. All six palette colors have been chosen to
        provide at least 3:1 contrast against white. Adding a
        stroke would make each avatar look like it has a "ring"
        that some users would interpret as an online indicator —
        confusing with the presence dot in the chat header.

    Why no <corners>:
      - <corners> is ignored on an <oval> shape (the oval's
        radius is derived from the bounds). Adding it would be
        harmless but would produce a lint warning. Leave it out.
    ============================================================
-->
<shape
    xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:shape="oval">

    <solid android:color="#FFFFFFFF" />

</shape>
